{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-133829",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The width of the plot is $x+1$ meters, and its area is $2{x}^{2}+2x$ square meters. Since the $\\text{area}=\\text{length}\\times \\text{width},$ we factor the expression for the area to find the length.\n\nFirst, we find the $\\text{GCF}$ of the two terms:\n\n$$\n\\text{GCF}(2{x}^{2},2x)=2x\n$$\n\nThen, we rewrite each term in the original expression as a product with the $\\text{GCF}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+2x & = \\\\ (2x\\cdot x)+(2x\\cdot 1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we write the $\\text{GCF}$ outside the parentheses and keep the remaining terms inside:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2x\\cdot x)+(2x\\cdot 1) & = \\\\ 2x(x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we conclude that the length of the plot must be $2x$ meters.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14168483/assets/4c27076821a19720b9038ab64552c85befa0c544e5295f1677b493ab36e6c9c9.png)",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
